module

public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.VeryAmpleAffineBase.CoordinateImmersion

/-!
# Very ample with finitely generated global sections implies locally of finite type

Let `S` be an affine scheme, `f : X ⟶ S` a morphism and `L` an invertible `𝒪_X`-module which is
`f`-relatively very ample. If the global sections `Γ(X, L)` are generated, as a module over
`Γ(S, 𝒪_S)`, by finitely many sections, then `f` is locally of finite type.

This is the finiteness the argument of [Stacks, Tag 01VO] rests on. There the source reasons
that very ampleness would make `X` a closed subscheme of a projective space over `k`. A relatively
very ample sheaf only gives an immersion into `𝐏(E)` for some quasi-coherent `E`, and `𝐏(E)` need
not be of finite type; it is the finiteness of `Γ(X, L)` that cuts the immersion down. Concretely,
let `α : X ⟶ Proj B` be the immersion, `B = Γ(S, Sym(E))` (generated in degree one), and
`s_y ∈ Γ(X, L)` the section attached to a degree-one element `y ∈ B`. Around any point of `X` the
overlap chart `W = α⁻¹(D₊(y₀ g))` is affine, `α` is a closed immersion over `D₊(y₀ g)`, and the
sections of `W` are generated over `Γ(S, 𝒪_S)` by the ratios `s_y / s_{y₀}` and one inverse. Each
`s_y` is a `Γ(S, 𝒪_S)`-combination of the finitely many generators `t ∈ T` of `Γ(X, L)`, so the
ratios `t / s_{y₀}` and the inverse already generate.

## Main results

* `Stacks.VeryAmpleFiniteSections.ratio_mem_closure`: a ratio `s / s₀` of global sections of an
  invertible module lies in the subring generated by the base sections and the ratios `t / s₀` of
  any finite family `T` generating the global sections over the base.
* `Stacks.VeryAmpleFiniteSections.locallyOfFiniteType_of_isRelativelyVeryAmple`: **a relatively
  very ample invertible module over an affine base with finitely generated global sections forces
  the morphism to be locally of finite type.**
* `Stacks.Tag01VMVeryAmple.IsRelativelyVeryAmple.of_iso`: relative very ampleness depends only on
  the isomorphism class of the invertible module.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 01VO](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01VO)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 01VM](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01VM)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 02NP](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02NP)
-/

@[expose] public section

noncomputable section

open AlgebraicGeometry CategoryTheory TopologicalSpace Opposite
